{
  "id": "valid-controlled-force-scaling",
  "kind": "valid",
  "title": "Valid controlled comparison — at fixed mass, more net force travels further",
  "equations": [
    "Trial 0: Fnet = 0 N, so a = Fnet/m = 0 m/s^2",
    "Trial 0: v(t) = v0 = 0 m/s, x(t) = x0 + v0*t = 0 m",
    "Trial 1: Fnet = 4 N, m = 2 kg, so a = Fnet/m = 2 m/s^2",
    "Trial 1: v(t) = v0 + a*t = 0 + 2*t, so v(3) = 6 m/s",
    "Trial 1: x(t) = x0 + v0*t + 0.5*a*t^2 = 0 + 0 + 0.5*2*t^2 = t^2, so x(3) = 9 m",
    "Trial 1 reaches the 4 m marker when t^2 = 4, i.e. t = 2 s"
  ],
  "rationale": "Both trials hold mass, initial velocity, and the observation span fixed; only the applied force changes, so the comparison isolates the effect of force. Trial 1 ends at 9.00 m having started from rest, which is more than double the 4.00 m it would reach under 2 N. The balanced trial is included deliberately: it is the control that shows the change is caused by the force and not by elapsed time.",
